KO-04-C5 Interrupted recovery - prove that recovery is idempotent

OS mechanism (what makes rerunning converge)
  * Truth comes only from verified bytes: CURRENT (own CRC) naming a version whose
    SHA-256 verifies; if CURRENT is missing/torn, the newest version that verifies.
    The journal is used only for cleanup and reporting, never as the truth.
  * Every step is a deterministic function of what is on disk: delete *.tmp (never
    committed by construction), delete versions ABOVE the truth (provably never
    committed), rewrite CURRENT only if it does not already name the truth.
    A step whose work is done finds nothing to do on the next run.
  * The decision is flushed (fsync version file, fsync CURRENT, fsync the doc
    directory, fsync docs/) BEFORE the checkpoint replaces the journal with a fresh
    salt via temp+fsync+rename+dir-fsync. A kill or power cut before the checkpoint
    leaves the old journal (rerun reports the same txs again); after it, nothing is
    left to do. So run N+1 starts from a state run N would also have produced.
  * Reports describe what THAT run did: rolled_back/rolled_forward come from the
    journal and repeat until the checkpoint resets it; fell_back is reported only
    by the run that actually repaired CURRENT. The on-disk result is what must
    (and does) converge.
Assumptions: local FS with atomic rename and honoured flushes; process kills keep
the page cache (Part A); power loss is simulated by SimFS (ALICE-style, rename model
W1) in Part B; faults are injected test data, A = 64 x 0x41, B = 64 x 0x42.

== Scenarios and the expected accepted version
   S1  v1=A committed + uncommitted v2=B + leftover temps (organisers' setup)
       expected: v1 = 64B of 0x41 ('A').  CURRENT is readable and names v1, whose SHA-256 verifies, so v1=A is the committed truth. v2 is above what CURRENT names: it was never committed (the crashed put died before its CURRENT rename), so it is rolled back. CURRENT is already right, so the pointer-rewrite step is never needed.
       reaches: recover_after_tmp_unlink=yes, recover_after_version_unlink=yes, recover_after_pointer_rewrite=no, recover_before_checkpoint=yes
   S2  S1 + CURRENT torn + v2 torn (simulated media faults)
       expected: v1 = 64B of 0x41 ('A').  CURRENT does not parse, so the rule is 'newest version whose SHA-256 verifies'. v2 is torn (fails length/SHA-256) and is not a whole version; v1=A verifies, so A is the truth: v2 is deleted and CURRENT is rewritten -> v1.
       reaches: recover_after_tmp_unlink=yes, recover_after_version_unlink=yes, recover_after_pointer_rewrite=yes, recover_before_checkpoint=yes
   S3  S1 + CURRENT torn, v2=B intact
       expected: v2 = 64B of 0x42 ('B').  CURRENT does not parse, so the commit record is lost and the store cannot prove v2 was NOT committed (a crash just after the CURRENT rename followed by a damaged CURRENT looks the same). The rule picks the newest version that fully verifies: v2=B, a whole version written and fsynced by put() and declared in the journal BEGIN v1->v2. Accepting B is atomic (never a mix); CURRENT is rewritten -> v2 and B is reported as rolled forward. Nothing is above v2, so the version-unlink step is never needed.
       reaches: recover_after_tmp_unlink=yes, recover_after_version_unlink=no, recover_after_pointer_rewrite=yes, recover_before_checkpoint=yes

== Which recovery crash points each scenario reaches (checked, not assumed)
   PASS S1 reaches exactly ['after tmp unlink', 'after version unlink', 'before checkpoint']  - observed {'recover_after_tmp_unlink': True, 'recover_after_version_unlink': True, 'recover_after_pointer_rewrite': False, 'recover_before_checkpoint': True}
   PASS S2 reaches exactly ['after tmp unlink', 'after version unlink', 'after CURRENT rewrite', 'before checkpoint']  - observed {'recover_after_tmp_unlink': True, 'recover_after_version_unlink': True, 'recover_after_pointer_rewrite': True, 'recover_before_checkpoint': True}
   PASS S3 reaches exactly ['after tmp unlink', 'after CURRENT rewrite', 'before checkpoint']  - observed {'recover_after_tmp_unlink': True, 'recover_after_version_unlink': False, 'recover_after_pointer_rewrite': True, 'recover_before_checkpoint': True}
   PASS every recovery crash point is exercised by some scenario

